  "conclusion": "Joint-gradient100 gives100exposures per problem with logicalbatch2 accumulatedphysical1. It does not produce robust training-input separation or heldout coding separation. Selected100 mean target probabilities .5301704/.5301375, meanloss .6363090; first-input PCgap .06217536 and second .06188387. Heldout selected100 gap-.00374429; best heldout loss at90. The same exposures individually gave much stronger first-input separation and stronger final second-input gap, but joint gradients change the optimization problem and effective per-example gradient weight. Compared with plan14, exposure count and averaging both change, so this is not a pure isolation of interference, sample-count or compute effects."
